WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.499
It's all about the tongue, man.
You stick it in their mouth,

2
00:00:06.416 --> 00:00:08.388
but not all the way.

3
00:00:08.456 --> 00:00:11.397
They like that?
Dude, they love it.

4
00:00:13.205 --> 00:00:15.804
What are you doing?
Climbing.

5
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:23.864
Just deep enough to lick her teeth:
Slow, one at a time,
like you're painting them.

6
00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:26.914
This girl Rachel, she's a junior.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:30.426
- She's experienced.
- Mom says I gotta be home by 5:00.

8
00:00:30.494 --> 00:00:34.940
Just chill, okay? And hop the fence
before someone sees you.

9
00:00:42.164 --> 00:00:47.477
Are you sure this is okay?
Dave's mom's a Realtor.
If it's for sale and empty, it's a clubhouse.

10
00:00:47.548 --> 00:00:51.857
And if you say anything, you're dead.

11
00:00:51.929 --> 00:00:57.173
I told them you were cool.
These are very serious dudes.

12
00:00:59.252 --> 00:01:01.315
Come on.

13
00:01:02.396 --> 00:01:04.699
Cub Scout meeting's
down the street, boys.
[Chuckles]

14
00:01:05.806 --> 00:01:08.245
[Grunting]

15
00:01:08.315 --> 00:01:10.618
What a loser.
[Laughing]
Lame.

16
00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:13.721
- Gabe's cool, man.
He's one of us.
- [Boy] He better be.

17
00:01:18.112 --> 00:01:20.243
Uh, I'm not thirsty.

18
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.230
- What's this?
- [Boy] Club business, little man.

19
00:01:25.302 --> 00:01:27.969
- What shall we ask?
- Put your hands on.

20
00:01:30.251 --> 00:01:35.701
O dark spirit, will any of us die
in the year to come?

21
00:01:40.953 --> 00:01:46.300
- You're moving it.
- Put your hands back. Now.

22
00:01:51.854 --> 00:01:54.019
Who among us will die?

23
00:02:01.786 --> 00:02:03.883
"G,"

24
00:02:07.605 --> 00:02:09.634
"A"-

25
00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:11.580
Oh, man.

26
00:02:13.223 --> 00:02:14.784
"B,"

27
00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:21.347
"E."

28
00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:24.755
[Dave]
"Gabe. '"
[Boy] That blows.

29
00:02:24.827 --> 00:02:29.170
[Coughing]

30
00:02:32.083 --> 00:02:34.648
That's it. We're going to the hospital.
[Continues Coughing]

31
00:02:34.724 --> 00:02:40.333
- No, Mom. I'm too tired.
- Come on, sweetie.
You've had a fever for almost a week now.

32
00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.405
[Coughing]

33
00:02:47.498 --> 00:02:49.767
- Ow.
- Oh, my God.

34
00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.747
Oh, Gabe.
Are you okay?

35
00:02:52.816 --> 00:02:56.350
Are you okay?
Baby, what's wrong?

36
00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.238
[Breathing Heavily]
I'm dying.

37
00:03:42.842 --> 00:03:47.755
[Coughing, Wheezes]

38
00:03:47.825 --> 00:03:50.994
[Cuddy]
Twelve-year-old male.
Spiking fever, congested chest,

39
00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:53.268
coughing up green sputum,
pain in breathing.

40
00:03:53.343 --> 00:03:58.587
Baffling, though I vaguely recall a disease
called "meunomia"? "Pneumania"?

41
00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:03.904
But his chest X-ray and C.T. Scan
show an atypical pattern for pneumonia.
Pneumonia! That's it.

42
00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:07.077
Just a guess here,
but his parents big donors?

43
00:04:07.154 --> 00:04:10.619
No infiltrate,
just enlarged hilar lymph nodes.

44
00:04:10.699 --> 00:04:14.198
Tiny unicorns goring his bronchial tubes
would be cooler,

45
00:04:14.277 --> 00:04:19.020
and the way you're ignoring the question-
wow, they're extremely big donors.

46
00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:23.937
He's not responding to cefuroxime,
his pulse ox is dropping much faster
than it should for pneumonia,

47
00:04:24.008 --> 00:04:26.003
and plus he's got
an odd little rash.

48
00:04:26.081 --> 00:04:29.489
Excessive irritation.
He's 12. He's on auto-stroke.
On his arm.

49
00:04:29.558 --> 00:04:33.160
Papular lesion, one centimeter,
on top of a long, thin scab.

50
00:04:33.238 --> 00:04:38.482
Ah, you need a dermatologist.
If it's dry, keep it wet.
If it's wet, keep it dry.

51
00:04:38.554 --> 00:04:41.758
If it's not supposed to be there, cut it off.
I never could master all that.

52
00:04:41.832 --> 00:04:44.431
There you are.

53
00:04:44.506 --> 00:04:47.846
Were you scared?
It's okay. You're home now.

54
00:04:47.918 --> 00:04:49.856
Fine.

55
00:04:49.924 --> 00:04:51.519
Cuddy.

56
00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:59.416
- As a special favor to you-
- No. Admit it. I got you with the rash.

57
00:04:59.488 --> 00:05:04.835
The rash is a total snooze,
unless it's connected to the pneumonia,

58
00:05:06.176 --> 00:05:09.174
then it's party time.

59
00:05:12.463 --> 00:05:15.997
Purulent sputum, dyspnea,
rhonchi bilaterally.

60
00:05:16.074 --> 00:05:19.482
What kind of pneumonia
causes this kind of rash?
Legionnaire's disease.

61
00:05:19.553 --> 00:05:22.996
Usually means industrial ventilation systems,
convention centers. He's 12 years old.

62
00:05:23.063 --> 00:05:26.335
Send off a urine antigen and check
if he's joined the Elks. Next?

63
00:05:26.408 --> 00:05:29.851
- Fungal.
- Excellent.
Maybe the lodge went spelunking.

64
00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:33.019
- Chlamydia pneumonia.
- Twelve-year-olds don't have sex.

65
00:05:33.096 --> 00:05:35.661
Their mistake.
[Sighs]

66
00:05:35.737 --> 00:05:38.941
That's it?
Other possibilities?

67
00:05:39.015 --> 00:05:44.465
- What if we're thinking about this backwards?
- The rash came first, caused the pneumonia. Nice.

68
00:05:44.532 --> 00:05:49.206
Rickettsial. Tick bite.
Nymphal ticks are out now.
They're bloodthirsty little bastards.

69
00:05:49.281 --> 00:05:52.986
- Rash would be more pustular.
- Not always,
and there's only one rash, which fits.

70
00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:57.267
- New Jersey, it's most likely Lyme disease.
- All right. Let's keep him on fluids...

71
00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:59.403
and the cefuroxime to be safe.

72
00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:02.478
Biopsy that rash.

73
00:06:02.556 --> 00:06:05.656
And take another history.
Even if we don't learn what's causing this,

74
00:06:05.733 --> 00:06:08.537
we definitely need to know
if twelve-year-olds are getting any action.

75
00:06:13.191 --> 00:06:16.291
- What are you doing now?
- We're collecting fluids from the rash.

76
00:06:16.368 --> 00:06:18.602
Why?
They know what they're doing, Jeffrey.

77
00:06:18.675 --> 00:06:23.121
Oh, great endorsement coming from
the woman who thought it was a nasty cold.
If I had had him last week-

78
00:06:23.189 --> 00:06:25.320
This isn't helping.

79
00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.037
We're checking to see
if your son has a tick bite.

80
00:06:30.111 --> 00:06:34.352
Gabe, have you been camping recently,
playing sports outside, anything like that?

81
00:06:34.426 --> 00:06:36.364
I'm not that good at sports.

82
00:06:36.432 --> 00:06:39.464
- No hanging out anywhere new,
strange places?
- We've been through this.

83
00:06:39.542 --> 00:06:42.814
- We don't let him run wild
through the neighborhood, right?
- [Gabe Coughs]

84
00:06:45.494 --> 00:06:48.937
Okay. This might be a bit delicate.

85
00:06:50.911 --> 00:06:53.909
- We need to know if you're sexually active.
- I beg your pardon?

86
00:06:53.988 --> 00:06:56.518
- Mr. Reilich, we need to-
- No.

87
00:06:58.368 --> 00:07:00.306
Nothing like that.

88
00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:02.814
[Coughing]

89
00:07:02.883 --> 00:07:06.087
[Coughing Continues]
We've gotta get another C.T. Scan,
check his lungs again.

90
00:07:06.161 --> 00:07:08.156
- Third floor, right?
- Yeah, Radiology is-

91
00:07:08.233 --> 00:07:11.003
Sorry, medical personnel only.

92
00:07:13.985 --> 00:07:16.926
Where's the machine?
Well, we passed it, actually.

93
00:07:16.995 --> 00:07:20.871
A little tension in the room back there.
Thought you could use a break.

94
00:07:20.941 --> 00:07:24.145
Your dad's a pretty... high-powered guy.

95
00:07:24.218 --> 00:07:29.793
Yeah. He was an air force test pilot.
Real Top Gun stuff.

96
00:07:29.869 --> 00:07:31.807
Flew Mach 3.

97
00:07:31.875 --> 00:07:33.870
Hard to deal with sometimes?

98
00:07:33.949 --> 00:07:37.517
Guess I'm more like my mom.
Listen.

99
00:07:39.065 --> 00:07:42.599
I promise to keep my mouth shut,
but I need to know.

100
00:07:42.677 --> 00:07:46.850
You definitely haven't had
any sexual contact
of any kind?

101
00:07:46.923 --> 00:07:48.861
I wish.
[Coughs]

102
00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:50.925
You know what?

103
00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:54.035
Girls like the cool guys now,
but give it a few years.

104
00:07:54.113 --> 00:07:56.552
They start liking the smart guys.

105
00:07:56.622 --> 00:07:58.925
- You'll be all right.
- [Coughs]

106
00:08:00.968 --> 00:08:03.133
Can you keep a secret?

107
00:08:04.213 --> 00:08:07.211
I have to. It's my job.

108
00:08:09.831 --> 00:08:12.772
I'm cursed. I'm not kidding.

109
00:08:12.841 --> 00:08:17.652
This seance thing the kids did?
It spelled out my name.

110
00:08:17.722 --> 00:08:20.059
Said I was gonna die.

111
00:08:20.131 --> 00:08:24.669
First name and last name?
We've got a Gabe upstairs
who's very old, very sick.

112
00:08:24.745 --> 00:08:28.621
No. It's me.
I have the worst luck.

113
00:08:28.691 --> 00:08:31.860
One time I broke this mirror,
[Wrapper Tearing]

114
00:08:31.935 --> 00:08:34.408
The next week
my parents got separated.

115
00:08:34.476 --> 00:08:37.383
My parents split up too.

116
00:08:37.453 --> 00:08:42.196
Every kid feels like it's his fault.
It never is.

117
00:08:42.267 --> 00:08:46.542
So, what about the rest of the stuff?
Any playing outside
your parents don't know about?

118
00:08:46.615 --> 00:08:48.553
Anything like that?

119
00:08:49.625 --> 00:08:52.326
Maybe you were somewhere
you weren't supposed to be?

120
00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.466
[Man On P.A.]
Triage nurse, pick up line 274.
[Sighs] Oh, man.

121
00:08:55.544 --> 00:08:59.318
Gabe, it's important.

122
00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:06.604
A secret club.
What's the secret- they're all morons?

123
00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:10.714
He fell on something in the attic,
scraped his arm,
got the rash the next day.

124
00:09:10.792 --> 00:09:13.892
Said it smelled really moldy up there.

125
00:09:13.969 --> 00:09:18.974
- [House] Fungal pneumonia
without the cave. Clever.
- I'm gonna get a sample.

126
00:09:20.424 --> 00:09:25.235
Dr. Chase,
you have a few moments?

127
00:09:28.014 --> 00:09:29.918
Sorry, I've gotta go.

128
00:09:33.098 --> 00:09:36.872
These young doctors.
It's like they don't care about people.

129
00:09:36.943 --> 00:09:40.717
- No manners.
- My fault, probably.

130
00:09:41.792 --> 00:09:46.603
That's an interesting accent
you have there. I'd say Czech...

131
00:09:46.674 --> 00:09:48.771
with about 30 years of Aussie.

132
00:09:48.847 --> 00:09:51.788
You have quite an ear.

133
00:09:51.857 --> 00:09:54.353
You're Chase's dad.

134
00:09:56.037 --> 00:10:00.038
Hard to miss-you know, the big hug
and how happy he was to see you.

135
00:10:09.681 --> 00:10:11.619
Need a consult.
With a patient.

136
00:10:11.688 --> 00:10:14.184
Urgent doctor stuff.

137
00:10:15.801 --> 00:10:20.144
Twenty-six-year-old male,
sudden loss of the ability to speak-

138
00:10:20.214 --> 00:10:23.782
Just because you got out of clinic duty
doesn't mean everybody did.
To his father.

139
00:10:23.859 --> 00:10:27.097
Differential diagnosis?
Chase?

140
00:10:27.171 --> 00:10:29.610
Dad swoops in,
Chase swoops out.

141
00:10:29.678 --> 00:10:32.585
Dad say why he was here?
See? You asked.

142
00:10:32.655 --> 00:10:35.961
Dad comes 5,000 miles,
you're more curious than Junior is.

143
00:10:36.032 --> 00:10:39.007
[Liquid Pouring]
Can'tjust be about the divorce.

144
00:10:39.075 --> 00:10:43.144
It's been 15 years, and Mom's
been dead for 10 of them.
You think Daddy murdered her?

145
00:10:43.222 --> 00:10:46.288
You want to get to the bottom of this,
you're doing it exactly right:

146
00:10:46.365 --> 00:10:51.039
Don't talk to the people involved.
Drag your buddy away from work
for some pointless speculation.

147
00:10:51.113 --> 00:10:53.678
You want to know how
two chemicals interact,
do you ask them?

148
00:10:53.755 --> 00:10:57.392
No. They're gonna lie through
their lying little chemical teeth.

149
00:10:57.467 --> 00:11:00.408
Throw them in a beaker
and apply heat.

150
00:11:00.477 --> 00:11:02.848
Even I don't like you.

151
00:11:02.918 --> 00:11:05.414
You know, words can hurt.

152
00:11:17.699 --> 00:11:19.762
[Door Opens]

153
00:11:19.839 --> 00:11:23.111
Whoa, whoa, whoa, hold on, guys.
I don't care that you're up here.

154
00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:28.863
- You a cop?
- Doctor. I'm treating Gabe Reilich.

155
00:11:28.935 --> 00:11:33.609
- He said he fell over near some pipes?
- Yeah, over there.

156
00:11:33.683 --> 00:11:36.590
- What, here?
- Yeah.

157
00:11:38.365 --> 00:11:43.678
- Any of you guys been feeling sick lately?
Rash, cold, anything?
- [Together] No.

158
00:11:47.795 --> 00:11:50.200
- [Chatter On Police Radio]
- [Vehicle Door Slams]

159
00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:54.044
Oh, crap. He is a cop!
Damn it!

160
00:12:08.595 --> 00:12:12.768
[Grunts, Groaning]

161
00:12:16.787 --> 00:12:20.059
He fell on it-
some weird kind of insulation.

162
00:12:20.132 --> 00:12:22.902
It's old.
House was built in the '60s.

163
00:12:22.974 --> 00:12:26.975
What's it made of?
Felt, fibers of-what, cotton?

164
00:12:27.054 --> 00:12:29.550
[House Laughs]

165
00:12:31.402 --> 00:12:33.841
Sorry. Forgotten how funny your dad was.

166
00:12:33.909 --> 00:12:37.546
- Not as funny as you.
- [Exhales]
High praise.

167
00:12:37.621 --> 00:12:41.497
I know how protective
kids can be of their parents.

168
00:12:45.747 --> 00:12:49.748
Not cotton. Animal hair.

169
00:12:51.098 --> 00:12:53.093
Get me the C.T. Scan.

170
00:12:57.451 --> 00:13:01.486
First, find the name of the company
that made the insulation,

171
00:13:01.564 --> 00:13:05.942
and second, tell me what I'm seeing
that makes me want to short their stock.

172
00:13:06.012 --> 00:13:09.181
[Foreman]
Uh, enlarged hilar lymph nodes.

173
00:13:09.256 --> 00:13:11.559
- [Cameron]
Peribronchial thickening.
- Pleural effusions.

174
00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:15.597
Less obvious, more scary.

175
00:13:15.676 --> 00:13:18.081
Well, the mediastinum
doesn't look right.

176
00:13:18.151 --> 00:13:20.647
Slightly widened.
[Sighs]

177
00:13:20.726 --> 00:13:23.222
Oh, God.

178
00:13:23.301 --> 00:13:25.900
It can be transmitted
through infected animal hair.

179
00:13:25.976 --> 00:13:30.650
But the Gram's stain would have shown-
No. The cefuroxime would have killed
some of it, clouded the result.

180
00:13:30.724 --> 00:13:33.528
We've gotta get this kid on levaquin.

181
00:13:35.607 --> 00:13:37.841
What does he have?

182
00:13:37.914 --> 00:13:42.486
Anthrax. This house belong
to old man Hussein?

183
00:13:45.873 --> 00:13:48.244
Maybe he is cursed.

184
00:13:50.989 --> 00:13:56.963
[Monitor Beeping]

185
00:13:58.046 --> 00:14:00.782
[Coughing Softly]

186
00:14:00.855 --> 00:14:05.164
Anthrax. So, what-you think
there were terrorists in that attic?

187
00:14:05.235 --> 00:14:07.469
It's a naturally occurring bacteria.

188
00:14:07.542 --> 00:14:09.913
- We believe it was in the insulation.
- How sick is he?

189
00:14:09.984 --> 00:14:15.662
Anthrax is very dangerous,
but we've caught it early. He's on levaquin.
It's the best antibiotic we have.

190
00:14:15.736 --> 00:14:19.373
Lisa, you buy this?
Jeff, you've helped
our hospital a lot.

191
00:14:19.447 --> 00:14:23.015
I wouldn't have assigned
Dr. House to your case
if I didn't have every confidence in him.

192
00:14:23.092 --> 00:14:25.064
- Then who's this guy?
- Jeffrey!

193
00:14:25.132 --> 00:14:29.099
There's all these weird diseases
that can cause a rash.
What about leishmaniasis or filariasis?

194
00:14:29.178 --> 00:14:32.244
Where'd you hear about those?
Internet. I did some research.

195
00:14:32.322 --> 00:14:35.959
Well, those are very rare conditions.
Oh, and anthrax
grows along the interstate?

196
00:14:36.033 --> 00:14:38.506
Leishmaniasis doesn't cause
pneumonia, and filariasis-

197
00:14:38.575 --> 00:14:41.105
Just look into everything.
This is my son, all right?

198
00:14:41.184 --> 00:14:45.357
And I'm gonna stay on top of you
until I know he's safe.
I wouldn't expect anything else.

199
00:14:49.209 --> 00:14:51.979
Everyone's a doctor.

200
00:14:52.052 --> 00:14:53.990
[Alarm Beeping]

201
00:14:54.058 --> 00:14:57.523
- [Beeping Continues]
- [Wheezing]

202
00:14:57.603 --> 00:15:00.977
Breathe, baby. It's okay.
Breathe. I need you to breathe.

203
00:15:01.047 --> 00:15:02.745
His breathing-
[Foreman]
It's on the inhale.

204
00:15:02.819 --> 00:15:05.486
What does that mean?
It means his airway's closing up.

205
00:15:05.561 --> 00:15:07.898
[Jeffrey]
Is the anthrax doin' it?

206
00:15:07.969 --> 00:15:12.347
All right. Hold still, Gabe.
This is gonna be a little uncomfortable.

207
00:15:17.099 --> 00:15:18.968
[Gabe Gasps]

208
00:15:19.038 --> 00:15:22.138
- [Coughs]
- [Cameron]
We've got you. It's okay.

209
00:15:24.655 --> 00:15:27.060
- Nodules in his throat.
- Airway's occluded?

210
00:15:27.131 --> 00:15:31.166
- We've got to intubate. Ativan!
- [Cameron] Pushing 3 c.c.'s.

211
00:15:37.062 --> 00:15:38.966
[Gabe Gags, Coughs]

212
00:15:40.874 --> 00:15:44.339
- Airway's too tight.
- What's wrong? What's wrong?
No, you're killing him!

213
00:15:44.419 --> 00:15:47.623
- Jeffrey! Let them do their job.
- Airway's too tight.
Get me a smaller tube.

214
00:15:47.696 --> 00:15:49.531
- We're traching.
- Smaller tube.

215
00:15:55.655 --> 00:15:57.593
- His lips are cyanotic.
- He's not breathing.

216
00:15:57.661 --> 00:16:00.260
Foreman, we're traching.
No. I can do it. I'll get it.

217
00:16:01.406 --> 00:16:05.111
- Foreman, you're not getting through.
- [Cuddy] He's not getting air.

218
00:16:05.186 --> 00:16:07.716
- We have to trach him right now.
- No, I can do it.

219
00:16:07.794 --> 00:16:09.196
[Chase]
Betadine.

220
00:16:14.315 --> 00:16:16.378
I'm doing this.

221
00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:20.759
Got it.

222
00:16:26.754 --> 00:16:29.125
[Instrument Beeps]

223
00:16:31.336 --> 00:16:34.470
He's okay.
[Sobbing]

224
00:16:37.589 --> 00:16:39.527
[House]
Allergic reaction to the antibiotics?

225
00:16:39.596 --> 00:16:43.803
I don't think so.
We switched him to rifampin,
and there was no change in the swelling.

226
00:16:43.876 --> 00:16:47.718
We should try another antibiotic.
You really think he's allergic
to two antibiotics?

227
00:16:47.789 --> 00:16:50.262
I wanna know
what Dr. Chase thinks.

228
00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:53.864
It's possible he's allergic-
Oh! I'm sorry. Not you.

229
00:16:53.941 --> 00:16:57.406
Understandable mistake.
The other Dr. Chase.

230
00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:05.777
Boy gets anthrax
and happens to be allergic
to two antibiotics.

231
00:17:05.846 --> 00:17:10.292
Hate to step on anybody's toes,
but is it possible your guys
got this one wrong?

232
00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:13.803
The rash is classic anthrax.
Except the color.

233
00:17:13.872 --> 00:17:16.573
The rash hasn't turned black yet.
No necrosis, no anthrax.

234
00:17:16.647 --> 00:17:20.146
Necrosis can theoretically take
as long as two weeks.

235
00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.300
Whoa! Whoa! Whoa!

236
00:17:24.372 --> 00:17:28.784
Guys, it's not a competition.
It's a diagnosis.

237
00:17:29.856 --> 00:17:32.660
Okay. Who thinks Junior wins?

238
00:17:34.371 --> 00:17:37.210
Four to one.
It's not anthrax.

239
00:17:37.281 --> 00:17:40.986
So we start over.
What's changed?
What do the nodules tell us?

240
00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:46.035
- Sarcoidosis.
- Excellent. Send an ACE level.

241
00:17:46.108 --> 00:17:50.680
If it comes back positive,
put him on methotrexate.

242
00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:55.636
[Foreman]
We ran some tests and the results
point toward sarcoidosis.

243
00:17:55.706 --> 00:17:58.407
It makes the body's
tissues swell up.

244
00:17:58.481 --> 00:18:02.323
It seems to have gone after
Gabriel's skin and lungs
and given him this fever.

245
00:18:02.394 --> 00:18:05.164
- [Mother] Doctor.
- [Tapping On Whiteboard]

246
00:18:07.878 --> 00:18:10.579
We don't know what causes it.

247
00:18:10.654 --> 00:18:14.598
But you're sure he has this one?
We have a world-renowned
doctor consulting.

248
00:18:14.667 --> 00:18:18.543
- He's convinced this is sarcoidosis.
- Is it treatable?

249
00:18:18.613 --> 00:18:24.291
Very, actually.
See, this is I.V. Methotrexate.
It's an anti-inflammatory.

250
00:18:24.365 --> 00:18:27.397
It should make all the swelling go down
and, uh, get you all better.

251
00:18:28.478 --> 00:18:30.575
Stop.

252
00:18:32.558 --> 00:18:34.553
My God, it's black.

253
00:18:36.503 --> 00:18:38.407
Necrosis.

254
00:18:38.477 --> 00:18:43.619
It's definitely anthrax,
and it definitely can't be anthrax.
It doesn't cause throat nodules.

255
00:18:43.693 --> 00:18:48.572
Only explanation is,
this kid's got anthrax and sarcoidosis.

256
00:18:48.642 --> 00:18:51.207
Right-
two incredibly rare diseases...

257
00:18:51.284 --> 00:18:54.818
- just happening to strike at once.
- Unless you've got a better theory.

258
00:18:54.896 --> 00:18:56.959
Anthrax plus an allergic reaction.

259
00:18:57.036 --> 00:19:00.570
Call the Lancet, because that's
one bizarre allergic reaction.

260
00:19:00.648 --> 00:19:04.182
Come on. There's no reason
you both can't be wrong.

261
00:19:04.258 --> 00:19:06.697
It's not allergy,
but it's not coincidence either.

262
00:19:06.767 --> 00:19:08.966
Disease number one set off
disease number two.

263
00:19:09.041 --> 00:19:12.951
- Anthrax weakened his immune system.
- [House] And triggered a dormant sarcoidosis.

264
00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:18.059
Keep him on antibiotics for the anthrax,
and start the methotrexate
for the sarcoidosis. See what happens.

265
00:19:18.136 --> 00:19:20.199
Fine.

266
00:19:22.952 --> 00:19:26.258
Better go with him.
Make sure he doesn't snap
and hurt somebody.

267
00:19:30.074 --> 00:19:32.273
Yeah, but y-you're treating him
for both diseases.

268
00:19:32.348 --> 00:19:36.521
We're covering all the bases.
What- throw everything against the wall
and see what sticks?

269
00:19:36.596 --> 00:19:38.534
Works for spaghetti.

270
00:19:39.973 --> 00:19:42.709
[Clears Throat]

271
00:19:42.782 --> 00:19:46.247
[Video Game Gunfire]
[Knocks]

272
00:19:47.999 --> 00:19:49.834
[Gunfire Stops]

273
00:19:54.687 --> 00:19:56.956
[Lock Snaps]

274
00:20:03.047 --> 00:20:06.684
A-Are you being funny?
Apparently not.

275
00:20:06.759 --> 00:20:09.198
You know why I give money
to this hospital?

276
00:20:09.267 --> 00:20:11.706
It's the only way
to get attention. See this?

277
00:20:11.775 --> 00:20:15.183
Is this a magic trick? 'Cause I am
a total David Copperfield fan.

278
00:20:15.253 --> 00:20:20.429
Although I thought that tornado of fire-
that seemed a little fake.
Pain in the wrist. It won't go away for months.

279
00:20:20.503 --> 00:20:25.679
Six doctors' brilliant conclusion
is to take it easy.
I write a check, name goes on a plaque,

280
00:20:25.753 --> 00:20:30.131
and 48 hours later I've got two M.R.I.'s,
a bone scan and a diagnosis: Carpal tunnel.

281
00:20:30.201 --> 00:20:33.142
I'm in surgery that afternoon.

282
00:20:33.211 --> 00:20:37.783
Fascinating story.
Have you thought
of adapting it for the stage?

283
00:20:37.859 --> 00:20:41.302
[Pager Beeping]
I love my- Look at me.
I love my son.

284
00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:45.611
I love him more than anything else in
the world. Start paying attention to this case,
or I'm gonna make things miserable-

285
00:20:45.683 --> 00:20:47.655
Go to your son's room.
Not until you get your ass-

286
00:20:47.724 --> 00:20:49.855
There's a problem.

287
00:20:56.351 --> 00:20:59.349
[Gabe Whimpering]

288
00:20:59.427 --> 00:21:03.201
- What is it?
- I don't know.

289
00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:06.877
[Whimpering Continues]

290
00:21:26.782 --> 00:21:29.085
[Foreman]
Skin lesions are spreading
all over his body.

291
00:21:29.156 --> 00:21:31.652
They're opening up,
and the fatty tissues are oozing out.

292
00:21:31.731 --> 00:21:36.440
He'll be septic in a matter of days.
[Sighs]
Death by dermatitis.

293
00:21:36.513 --> 00:21:40.012
- Where's Robert?
- Uh, he has clinic duty this morning.

294
00:21:40.091 --> 00:21:46.065
No, he doesn't.
I rescheduled you guys so you'd be free.
Yeah, but he re-rescheduled himself.

295
00:21:53.066 --> 00:21:55.596
It doesn't hurt yet.
Keep going.

296
00:21:55.674 --> 00:21:58.147
You page me?

297
00:21:58.216 --> 00:22:00.154
No, I don't need you.
Come on.

298
00:22:00.222 --> 00:22:03.026
We all need help now and again.
You're getting a consult.

299
00:22:04.235 --> 00:22:09.377
- Okay. We've got new skin lesions,
bigger and uglier. What would cause that?
- My hand hurts.

300
00:22:09.452 --> 00:22:14.092
What if his body worked so hard attacking
the anthrax that it started attacking itself?

301
00:22:14.167 --> 00:22:16.538
- Autoimmune.
- Wouldn't present this aggressively.

302
00:22:16.607 --> 00:22:20.882
- It's not likely, but it is possible.
- What, in a twelve-year-old male?

303
00:22:20.955 --> 00:22:25.800
- This isn't about me, is it?
- Gabe's dad found leishmaniasis and filariasis
on the Internet yesterday.

304
00:22:25.871 --> 00:22:28.937
- They didn't fit then,
but now they kind of do.
- Sure,

305
00:22:29.014 --> 00:22:32.218
except for the nodules, and we're
not working out of Calcutta General.

306
00:22:32.291 --> 00:22:35.756
- Multiple neurofibromatosis.
- You think this is neurological?

307
00:22:35.836 --> 00:22:39.404
The only reason you're thinking autoimmune
is because you're a rheumatologist.

308
00:22:39.481 --> 00:22:42.148
If you were a proctologist,
you'd think rectal cancer.

309
00:22:42.223 --> 00:22:44.662
Gotta go with Senior.

310
00:22:44.731 --> 00:22:47.797
- He literally wrote the book on this one.
- Autoimmune is a big universe.

311
00:22:47.874 --> 00:22:50.849
It could be anything
from scleroderma to Churg-Strauss.

312
00:22:50.918 --> 00:22:53.551
Whatever it is,
we should start him on steroids,
keep the swelling down.

313
00:22:53.626 --> 00:22:56.362
A hundred milligrams of cytoxan-
it treats most autoimmunes.

314
00:22:56.435 --> 00:22:59.900
Well, give it to him now before the fat
starts dripping out his eyeballs.

315
00:23:01.919 --> 00:23:05.191
Hey, my fingers are numb.

316
00:23:05.264 --> 00:23:07.327
Your watch is on too tight.

317
00:23:10.614 --> 00:23:12.609
[Door Closes]

318
00:23:15.429 --> 00:23:20.571
You're messing with my head.
Your relationship with your dad is
messing with your ability to do your job.

319
00:23:20.647 --> 00:23:23.679
Only because you made
my dad part of my job.
Good point.

320
00:23:23.756 --> 00:23:27.164
You haven't seen him in years,
he flies across the Atlantic to see you-
Pacific.

321
00:23:27.234 --> 00:23:32.239
- You breeze by him like he's a Hare Krishna
at the airport, don't ask why he's in town.
- It's probably a conference.

322
00:23:32.317 --> 00:23:35.258
Probably.

323
00:23:35.327 --> 00:23:40.206
I was hoping to do this
through sheer manipulation,
but if you insist on talking, fine. Talk.

324
00:23:42.215 --> 00:23:46.490
- What did he do to you?
- How would you feel if I interfered
in your personal life?

325
00:23:47.566 --> 00:23:50.804
I'd hate it- that's why
I cleverly have no personal life.

326
00:23:50.876 --> 00:23:53.372
I'm gonna biopsy
his skin lesions.

327
00:23:53.451 --> 00:23:58.958
Good thinking. Prove your dad wrong.
[Quieter]
That'll solve everything.

328
00:24:01.477 --> 00:24:03.472
[Printer Beeps]

329
00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:18.121
Who extubated him?
I did.

330
00:24:18.197 --> 00:24:21.571
Temperature's down two degrees,
and the swelling's almost gone.

331
00:24:21.641 --> 00:24:23.579
And his skin looks better too.

332
00:24:23.647 --> 00:24:26.314
Cytoxan is working.

333
00:24:26.390 --> 00:24:29.628
- Feeling better, Gabe?
- Yep, a lot.

334
00:24:29.701 --> 00:24:32.905
[Chase]
Dr. Chase, can you and I have a word?

335
00:24:36.288 --> 00:24:39.195
Your diagnosis is wrong.
No autoimmune disease.

336
00:24:39.265 --> 00:24:43.939
The swelling's probably just down
because we've got him on steroids.
It's masking whatever's really wrong.

337
00:24:44.013 --> 00:24:49.691
A.N.A.'s are unreliable.
Phospholipid antibodies are negative,
so no lupus. Same for Churg-Strauss.

338
00:24:49.765 --> 00:24:51.703
You're arguing
with a rheumatologist.

339
00:24:51.771 --> 00:24:55.806
There's about 20 distinct
autoimmune diseases-
Why are you here?

340
00:24:58.392 --> 00:25:00.387
S.L.E. Conference.

341
00:25:00.466 --> 00:25:04.707
You were in New York last year
for the scleroderma conference.
I didn't hear anything from you.

342
00:25:04.779 --> 00:25:08.814
Just wanted to say hi this time.
You said it.
You're still here.

343
00:25:10.030 --> 00:25:11.968
I miss you.

344
00:25:13.039 --> 00:25:17.645
I was 15 years old
when you walked out.
Now you're walking back in?

345
00:25:17.721 --> 00:25:20.662
I left your mother.
I didn't leave you.

346
00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:25.838
Mum was living on gin and tonics.
How was I supposed to take care of her?
She wasn't your responsibility.

347
00:25:25.914 --> 00:25:29.048
I know! She was yours.

348
00:25:31.064 --> 00:25:35.977
I'm sorry she died.
I'm sorry you had to deal with that.

349
00:25:37.317 --> 00:25:40.018
But she was falling apart
long before-

350
00:25:40.093 --> 00:25:43.000
I've got to talk to House
about this treatment.

351
00:25:47.817 --> 00:25:50.553
But the patient's getting better.

352
00:25:50.626 --> 00:25:52.564
In spite of the cytoxan.

353
00:25:52.633 --> 00:25:56.235
On the other hand- getting better.

354
00:25:56.311 --> 00:25:59.252
Cytoxan makes him more
susceptible to infection.

355
00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:02.592
The anthrax could relapse
and be more resistant.
Better!

356
00:26:02.665 --> 00:26:06.005
You want a negative test
for every autoimmune disease
known to man?

357
00:26:06.075 --> 00:26:11.022
- Fine. I'll get 'em.
- Be home by midnight
or you can't have the car this weekend.

358
00:26:12.997 --> 00:26:15.995
You guys talk?
Did he tell you why he's here?

359
00:26:17.077 --> 00:26:19.573
The S.L.E. Conference.

360
00:26:31.758 --> 00:26:36.330
Going back to the conference?
Afternoon panel.
I hope I can stay awake.

361
00:26:36.406 --> 00:26:40.613
I hope you can get in.
You're not registered.

362
00:26:44.599 --> 00:26:48.600
I get it. You had to make up a lie.

363
00:26:48.679 --> 00:26:51.654
Can't just tell your kid
you're here to see him.

364
00:26:51.722 --> 00:26:55.290
I mean, what father does that?

365
00:26:56.370 --> 00:27:01.979
That little blue dot under your collar-
it's a tattoo for guiding radiation treatment.

366
00:27:03.058 --> 00:27:07.093
I was looking for it after I saw
what you had for breakfast:
Brown rice, vegetables.

367
00:27:07.171 --> 00:27:13.053
It's a macrobiotic diet-
popular with Hollywood starlets
and... cancer patients.

368
00:27:15.364 --> 00:27:17.393
Lungs. Stage IV.

369
00:27:19.244 --> 00:27:22.778
- You look good.
- I'm not.

370
00:27:22.854 --> 00:27:27.597
Came to the States
to go to Sloan-Kettering
and see Dr. Wilson.

371
00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:31.739
- What'd he say?
- Three months.

372
00:27:31.817 --> 00:27:33.789
But you haven't told Robert.

373
00:27:33.857 --> 00:27:38.132
You don't want to burden him,
because you were such a lousy dad.

374
00:27:40.377 --> 00:27:42.646
I'd prefer you not tell him.

375
00:27:44.357 --> 00:27:46.295
Yeah. That's better.

376
00:27:47.869 --> 00:27:50.399
I'll get to see his face
when he reads his dad's obituary.

377
00:27:50.476 --> 00:27:53.109
It's not your business.

378
00:27:55.693 --> 00:27:57.824
I suppose it isn't.

379
00:28:06.695 --> 00:28:08.758
You sure do a lot of tests.

380
00:28:08.835 --> 00:28:13.840
If we figure out exactly
which autoimmune condition it is,
we can get you better quicker.

381
00:28:13.918 --> 00:28:17.726
Was that your dad before?
Yeah.

382
00:28:17.798 --> 00:28:21.104
That's so cool.
Do you guys work together a lot?

383
00:28:21.175 --> 00:28:23.842
Not really.

384
00:28:23.917 --> 00:28:26.048
When can I go home?

385
00:28:26.124 --> 00:28:28.393
We'll see.
[Removing Rubber Gloves]

386
00:28:28.465 --> 00:28:32.740
Looks like you got your appetite back.
That's a good sign.
[Coughing]

387
00:28:32.812 --> 00:28:35.548
Want some water?
Yes.
[Continues Coughing]

388
00:28:37.795 --> 00:28:40.827
- Ah!
- Here you go.

389
00:28:40.905 --> 00:28:43.572
[Cup Hits Floor]

390
00:28:43.647 --> 00:28:46.781
Whoops.
That's all right. Water's cheap.

391
00:28:46.857 --> 00:28:50.197
- Oh, God.
- What?

392
00:28:52.174 --> 00:28:54.841
I can't-
I can't move my hand.

393
00:29:03.778 --> 00:29:06.776
[Chase]
Can you squeeze my fingers
for me, Gabe?

394
00:29:11.904 --> 00:29:15.541
[Chase]
He's getting worse. Now his entire
right hand and forearm are paralyzed.

395
00:29:15.616 --> 00:29:20.792
And his fever's back. It's up over 105.
If we don't stop the nerve deterioration
quickly, he'll be paralyzed for life.

396
00:29:20.866 --> 00:29:24.571
Well, luckily, at this rate
that should only last about a week.

397
00:29:24.645 --> 00:29:28.110
- Okay. So-
- I told you we should
get him off the cytoxan.

398
00:29:28.190 --> 00:29:32.967
This is toxic neuropathy.
We've been shoving drugs into his system
for a disease he doesn't have.

399
00:29:33.038 --> 00:29:37.917
- Well, it could be neurological.
- What kind of brain process
would cause a paralyzed hand,

400
00:29:37.987 --> 00:29:40.517
skin lesions and swollen throat nodules?

401
00:29:40.596 --> 00:29:45.840
Robert was right.
You said multiple neurofibromatosis.

402
00:29:45.913 --> 00:29:50.291
Are you saying that for the chance
at a beautiful family moment,
or is there some medicine involved?

403
00:29:50.361 --> 00:29:52.630
- Mm-mmm. Fits better.
- Too bad.

404
00:29:52.701 --> 00:29:56.806
I was hoping for the other reason.
I was gonna go get my camera.

405
00:29:56.881 --> 00:29:59.720
Get a C.T. Scan- his brain this time.

406
00:30:07.783 --> 00:30:10.690
You'd think three copies would
be enough, right?

407
00:30:10.759 --> 00:30:13.963
One's always been
more than enough for me.

408
00:30:14.036 --> 00:30:17.308
Why didn't you tell me
that Rowan Chase was in to see you?

409
00:30:18.383 --> 00:30:20.754
Excuse me.

410
00:30:20.825 --> 00:30:24.393
Ethics, confidentiality-
does any of this ring a bell?

411
00:30:24.470 --> 00:30:29.315
You could have covered yourself,
called me in for a consult.
It is a juicy piece of gossip.

412
00:30:29.386 --> 00:30:33.729
You know what happened?
I got all focused on his cancer
and lost perspective.

413
00:30:33.799 --> 00:30:36.899
You can't tell Chase, but I can.
What should I do?

414
00:30:36.976 --> 00:30:41.319
This is where I give you advice
and pretend you're gonna listen to it.
I like this part.

415
00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:44.297
Did Rowan ask you not to tell?
I promised I wouldn't.

416
00:30:44.367 --> 00:30:46.396
My fingers were crossed,
though, so I'm wide open.

417
00:30:46.473 --> 00:30:51.147
I was wrong. This is the musing-out-loud part.
Do I actually need to be here?
Telling him.

418
00:30:51.222 --> 00:30:55.030
Now, that's got real
entertainment value.
Mm. He might even cry.

419
00:30:55.101 --> 00:30:57.837
On the other hand, there is
the do-unto-others thingy.

420
00:30:57.909 --> 00:31:01.910
Well, then I should definitely tell him.
I'd wanna know.
You wanna know everything.

421
00:31:01.989 --> 00:31:04.725
There's also
the keeping-your-promises thingy.

422
00:31:04.799 --> 00:31:09.302
Oh, you never run out of thingies-
like that blonde thingy
you were chatting up.

423
00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:13.051
She's the hospital accountant.
We were going over billing procedures.

424
00:31:13.125 --> 00:31:16.294
Double-entry bookkeeping?

425
00:31:18.543 --> 00:31:20.481
What are you gonna do?

426
00:31:23.224 --> 00:31:26.290
Billing procedures.
They're so complicated, aren't they?

427
00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:38.062
[Coughing]

428
00:31:38.138 --> 00:31:40.133
[Machinery Thumps, Beeps]

429
00:31:43.824 --> 00:31:47.529
You know, parents are never as bad
as kids think they are.

430
00:31:47.602 --> 00:31:51.512
If you like my dad so much,
ask him out.

431
00:31:51.582 --> 00:31:54.523
I'd make an excellent stepmom.
I'm very lenient.

432
00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:03.818
He's your father.
You never see him, and he's here.

433
00:32:03.888 --> 00:32:07.387
Unless he's done
some unspeakable thing,
you should lighten up.

434
00:32:07.466 --> 00:32:09.735
Great. Thanks for the tip.

435
00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:12.748
Okay.

436
00:32:14.355 --> 00:32:17.524
He beat your mom.
He beat you.

437
00:32:18.534 --> 00:32:21.600
What did he do?
Really, don't push it.

438
00:32:26.292 --> 00:32:30.134
All this hate. It's toxic.

439
00:32:31.911 --> 00:32:33.849
Then stay away.

440
00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:38.659
[Breathes Deeply]

441
00:32:42.344 --> 00:32:45.011
There's nothing there.

442
00:32:45.087 --> 00:32:49.556
No masses, no fibrous tangles, nothing.

443
00:32:49.634 --> 00:32:51.606
It's not neurofibromatosis.

444
00:32:51.674 --> 00:32:55.915
Drs. Chase are 0 for 3.
Even when they agree,
they're wrong.

445
00:32:55.988 --> 00:33:00.958
So, next, what else could cause neural damage
as well as all the nodules?
[Cameron] Berger's disease.

446
00:33:01.037 --> 00:33:04.411
- He's never been out of the country.
- Have his parents?
They could have brought it back.

447
00:33:04.481 --> 00:33:08.756
- I don't think so.
- It doesn't matter. His lesions are in the
wrong place. His feet are the one clear spot.

448
00:33:08.829 --> 00:33:12.363
- Kid's dad mentioned leishmaniasis.
- Yeah, and filariasis.

449
00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:17.718
- But the throat nodules still don't fit with that.
- That's two diseases pretty much
exclusive to Southeast Asia.

450
00:33:22.272 --> 00:33:26.182
What if the anthrax
didn't set off the second disease?

451
00:33:26.251 --> 00:33:31.256
- We're back at coincidence again?
- No. What if something else
was the trigger?

452
00:33:31.335 --> 00:33:33.273
- Nothing else happened.
- We happened.

453
00:33:33.341 --> 00:33:36.784
- Antibiotics?
- We've been through this.
It's not an allergy.

454
00:33:38.457 --> 00:33:40.452
I gotta pee.

455
00:33:44.276 --> 00:33:49.281
[Monitor Beeping Softly]

456
00:33:49.358 --> 00:33:51.353
[Door Sliding Open]

457
00:33:54.709 --> 00:33:58.916
- What did the C.T. Scan tell you?
- Nothing.

458
00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:01.965
Ow!
Scale from one to 10-
how painful?

459
00:34:02.032 --> 00:34:05.942
Not half as painful
as when I punch you in the face.
Don't do that. It'll hurt you.

460
00:34:06.012 --> 00:34:09.683
- Carpal tunnel surgery
obviously didn't work.
- Who are you?

461
00:34:09.757 --> 00:34:14.865
The little ones call me Uncle Greg.
Your dad never had carpal tunnel.

462
00:34:14.941 --> 00:34:19.046
You mentioned
two obscure diseases to Dr. Chase.
How did you know about them?

463
00:34:19.121 --> 00:34:24.263
- I read about them on the Internet.
- What did you search for- " Diseases from Asia
that don't match my son's symptoms"?

464
00:34:24.337 --> 00:34:26.571
- You head about them in Asia.
- I've never been there.

465
00:34:26.645 --> 00:34:29.745
Well, you probably just forgot.
Let me refresh your memory:

466
00:34:29.822 --> 00:34:34.234
Some remote, dusty village,
close quarters, at least a year?

467
00:34:34.302 --> 00:34:36.467
- Starting to come back?
- I'm calling Dr. Cuddy.

468
00:34:36.543 --> 00:34:39.142
Excuse me, what does this
have to do with our son?

469
00:34:39.218 --> 00:34:42.456
Your dad's pissed off. He should be.

470
00:34:42.529 --> 00:34:47.499
Comes here expecting us to do
an extra-good job because he gives
a whole lot of money to this hospital.

471
00:34:47.579 --> 00:34:50.748
- Don't talk to my son like that!
- Just telling him my job and my obligation-

472
00:34:50.822 --> 00:34:54.596
Stop!
What's going on?

473
00:34:58.079 --> 00:35:01.487
There's only one thing
you guys have gotta do.

474
00:35:03.095 --> 00:35:07.404
Tell the truth, or your son will die.

475
00:35:09.147 --> 00:35:12.179
How long were you in Asia?

476
00:35:15.301 --> 00:35:17.500
Jeff, it's a simple question.

477
00:35:22.056 --> 00:35:24.586
Two years, in India.

478
00:35:26.034 --> 00:35:29.272
Why would you lie
about something like that?
It was '87 and '88.

479
00:35:34.963 --> 00:35:38.337
This, uh-

480
00:35:38.408 --> 00:35:42.079
This guru-
l-I thought he had some answers.

481
00:35:43.156 --> 00:35:46.930
I went to his ashram...

482
00:35:47.002 --> 00:35:51.813
and, um, ended up with no money
and no answers.

483
00:35:53.456 --> 00:35:56.397
I was embarrassed.
I didn't want anyone to know.

484
00:35:57.804 --> 00:35:59.742
No.

485
00:36:01.615 --> 00:36:04.248
No. You were a test pilot.

486
00:36:04.324 --> 00:36:07.094
I'm so sorry, Gabe.

487
00:36:08.839 --> 00:36:11.609
What does this have to do with my son?

488
00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:23.173
Clue number one: If I wereJesus,
curing this kid would be as easy
as turning water into wine.

489
00:36:23.252 --> 00:36:26.957
Demonic possession?
Close, but no wafer.

490
00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:32.982
Clue number two:
Rheumatology Rowan was almost right.
It causes autoimmune symptoms.

491
00:36:33.049 --> 00:36:36.492
- Leprosy?
- Yeah, that's real big
in theJersey suburbs.

492
00:36:37.564 --> 00:36:41.098
It's leprosy.
Run a Fite stain. It'll be positive.

493
00:36:41.175 --> 00:36:44.549
Daddy hung out on
the wrong kind of Indian ashram.

494
00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:47.652
But it's obviously dormant in the dad.
How could the kid catch it?

495
00:36:47.730 --> 00:36:50.169
It's not dormant in the dad.
It's just slow.

496
00:36:50.238 --> 00:36:53.806
Damaged his ulnar nerves.
Was misdiagnosed as carpal tunnel.

497
00:36:53.883 --> 00:36:57.850
Never trust doctors.
Run a Fite stain.

498
00:36:57.929 --> 00:37:01.998
No wonder he got anthrax.
The leprosy weakened his immune system.

499
00:37:02.075 --> 00:37:05.609
Vicious circle: The leprosy
made him vulnerable
to the anthrax,

500
00:37:05.687 --> 00:37:08.058
and the anthrax treatment
put the leprosy in overdrive.

501
00:37:08.129 --> 00:37:13.841
- But the antibiotics we gave him-
they cure leprosy.
- Yeah. That's where the trouble starts.

502
00:37:13.913 --> 00:37:16.614
The antibiotics hit the nerve strands.

503
00:37:16.689 --> 00:37:20.394
They kill the leprosy bacteria,
and the corpses get tossed into the system.

504
00:37:20.468 --> 00:37:23.740
And as fascinating as our bodies are,
they're also stupid.

505
00:37:23.812 --> 00:37:27.917
They produce antibodies
to beat dead bacteria.

506
00:37:27.992 --> 00:37:30.591
And these aren't the polite antibodies.

507
00:37:30.667 --> 00:37:33.699
They're the ones that won't sit still,
kick during nap time.

508
00:37:33.777 --> 00:37:36.148
They attack his neural and fat cells,

509
00:37:36.218 --> 00:37:39.717
causing inflammation
and all the rest ofhis symptoms.

510
00:37:39.796 --> 00:37:42.065
So the cure's killing him.

511
00:37:42.137 --> 00:37:47.016
I want you to call down to Carville, Louisiana,
home of the last leper colony in the Lower 48.

512
00:37:47.086 --> 00:37:49.217
Get 'em to send up
some thalidomide.

513
00:37:49.293 --> 00:37:52.736
- Thalidomide?
- Twelve-year-olds don't have sex, right?
So he can't be pregnant.

514
00:37:52.805 --> 00:37:54.800
Make the call.

515
00:37:58.289 --> 00:38:00.922
I need to speak to your boy.

516
00:38:02.636 --> 00:38:04.197
Hmm.

517
00:38:13.337 --> 00:38:16.836
Why does everybody
need to know my business?

518
00:38:16.915 --> 00:38:19.651
People like talking about people.

519
00:38:19.724 --> 00:38:23.566
Makes us feel superior,
makes us feel in control,

520
00:38:23.637 --> 00:38:28.311
and sometimes, for some people,
knowing some things makes them care.

521
00:38:28.385 --> 00:38:33.561
I tell you my dad left,
my mum drank herself to death,
you gonna care about me more?

522
00:38:33.635 --> 00:38:37.511
Cameron would.
Me, I just like knowing stuff.

523
00:38:39.321 --> 00:38:42.559
[Sighs]
I know you hate your dad.

524
00:38:42.631 --> 00:38:44.900
- Now, I'm gonna tell you something-
- I don't hate him.

525
00:38:46.343 --> 00:38:51.222
I loved him until I figured out
that it hurts a lot less to just not care.

526
00:38:52.295 --> 00:38:56.137
You don't expect him to turn up
to your football match,
no disappointments.

527
00:38:56.208 --> 00:39:02.182
You don't expect a call on your birthday,
don't expect to see him for months,
no disappointments.

528
00:39:03.498 --> 00:39:08.913
You want us to go make up-
sink a few beers together, nice family hug?

529
00:39:08.982 --> 00:39:10.977
[Chuckles]

530
00:39:12.059 --> 00:39:17.440
I've given him enough hugs.
He's given me enough disappointments.

531
00:39:21.856 --> 00:39:24.193
Okay.

532
00:39:26.605 --> 00:39:30.013
- That's it?
- That's it.

533
00:39:36.837 --> 00:39:41.044
[Door Opens, Closes]

534
00:39:54.194 --> 00:39:56.964
- [Chase]
Breathe.
- [Exhales]

535
00:39:58.875 --> 00:40:01.941
Breathe again.
[Exhales]

536
00:40:02.018 --> 00:40:04.013
Breathe again.

537
00:40:04.091 --> 00:40:08.035
[Exhales]
Are you gonna have
to tell my friends I'm a leper?

538
00:40:08.104 --> 00:40:11.512
You had the antibiotics.
You're not contagious anymore.

539
00:40:11.582 --> 00:40:14.614
[Exhales]
Well, what if they already caught it?

540
00:40:14.692 --> 00:40:19.800
Leprosy is incredibly hard to catch.
Even your mum didn't get it.

541
00:40:19.875 --> 00:40:23.580
Right. I want you to make a fist.

542
00:40:23.655 --> 00:40:25.684
It's still kinda stiff.

543
00:40:25.761 --> 00:40:30.070
It'll get back to normal
in a month or two.
Your skin, two or three weeks.

544
00:40:30.142 --> 00:40:32.672
[Woman On P. A.]
Security 12, call 3662.

545
00:40:32.750 --> 00:40:35.919
You haven't asked about your dad.

546
00:40:37.465 --> 00:40:39.961
He lied about everything.

547
00:40:40.040 --> 00:40:44.418
He lied to my mom.
He lied to his boss.

548
00:40:44.487 --> 00:40:46.482
He's just a liar.

549
00:40:47.999 --> 00:40:49.937
He loves you.

550
00:40:52.078 --> 00:40:54.643
I don't love him.

551
00:40:56.158 --> 00:40:58.323
Yeah, you do.

552
00:40:58.399 --> 00:41:00.337
[Chuckles]

553
00:41:03.616 --> 00:41:07.685
Nothing you can do about it.
He's your dad.

554
00:41:07.762 --> 00:41:11.227
It doesn't matter what he does.
You're gonna love him.

555
00:41:24.315 --> 00:41:26.811
Here. Thanks.

556
00:41:28.228 --> 00:41:30.223
Hey.!

557
00:41:41.035 --> 00:41:43.508
When's your flight?
You got time for a drink?

558
00:41:44.580 --> 00:41:46.609
Wish I did.

559
00:41:46.687 --> 00:41:49.628
- [Car Door Closes]
- [Man]
Dr. Chase?

560
00:41:54.847 --> 00:41:56.785
[Clears Throat]

561
00:41:58.825 --> 00:42:00.820
I got it.

562
00:42:03.574 --> 00:42:06.914
You're gonna be gettin' down
to Oz anytime soon?

563
00:42:08.590 --> 00:42:11.588
Not too long.
Next autumn, I hope.

564
00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:15.611
I'll call ya.

565
00:42:17.686 --> 00:42:20.353
Well, you're all set.

566
00:42:28.654 --> 00:42:32.428
I'll see ya.
Yeah.

567
00:42:32.500 --> 00:42:34.438
I'll see you.

568
00:42:47.515 --> 00:42:50.182
[Car Engine Starts]

569
00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:54.292
[Door Opens, Closes]

570
00:43:42.457 --> 00:43:43.721
That's some bad hat, Harry.

9999
00:00:0.500 --> 00:00:2.00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>

